schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: 'What is dMRV and Why Should You Care?'
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: concept
  summary: >-
    A beginner-friendly introduction to Digital Measurement, Reporting, and
    Verification (dMRV) — proving that regenerative work (tree planting, water
    conservation, grazing-land recovery) really happened, using phones/apps
    for data collection and either automated (satellite) or human verification.
    Walks through worked examples from Hargeisa and Berbera, a simple four-step
    process, and a five-step getting-started guide using tools like
    KoboToolbox, ODK, or Google Forms.
  audience: Researcher / grassroots practitioners (beginner)
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      simple-phone-based-data-collection vs the technical/carbon-market
      expectations of formal MRV standards — the article deliberately keeps the
      bar low ("you don't need expensive equipment") while still connecting to
      "carbon markets and environmental programs" that require verified data.
  related_concepts:
    - digital-measurement-reporting-verification
  related_resources:
    - KoboToolbox
    - ODK
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  high_risk: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/dmrv.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-0e8b32eb0398
  source_lineage: dmrv.md
  notes: >
    # assessed-but-excluded

    - "Fatima's group in Hargeisa" and "a women's cooperative in Berbera" are
      described anonymously (first name only / no surname, no identifying
      detail beyond location + activity) — treated as illustrative composite
      examples, not real named individuals to profile; no public person-node
      created per the ingest skill's guardrail against creating public
      person-nodes. The named locations (Hargeisa, Berbera) combined with
      specific project outcomes are a high-risk trigger (exact locations +
      ecological/impact claims) — see the claim-evidence and
      public-use-boundary objects below.
